  "slug": "docking-dynamics-049f",
  "summary": "Seven new items probe what protein language models, foundation embeddings and prior-informed predictors capture, and where dataset properties limit performance.",
  "synthesis": "The items are heterogeneous. The dominant thread is critical evaluation of learned and statistical representations of biological sequence and perturbation data, rather than structure-based ligand engagement as such. Protein language model contact maps collapse under bijective amino-acid relabeling, unlike Potts and inverse-covariance methods. In OneProt-based allosteric versus orthosteric site classification, dataset properties dominate over encoder architecture. For perturbation response, target-level predictability tracks agreement among eight prior-knowledge sources in Perturb-seq, and S1Pert targets unseen genetic perturbations. Other preprints address epistasis: amino acid clustering for phenotype prediction, and Fisher exact and DISCOVER screens on 141 E. coli ALE experiments. MOTET jointly models SBS, DBS and ID signatures across 2,583 PCAWG genomes. Evidence is almost entirely preprint-level.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-16"
}
